-- title:
--   Sum of p-diamond operators kills the norm-free subscheme's special fibre
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a level $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, and a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ L$ which is equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (hypothesis `hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (hypothesis `hζA`), with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $A \to L \to K$, and let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ L$ is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular invariant (hypothesis `hj`). All the geometry below takes place over the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) of this function field over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper.
--
--   *Special fibre data.* Let $k$ be an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, given together with closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of $C_1$, $C_2$ over $k$ into the base change of the model to $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` requires that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` requires the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ to be reduced; $n$ is its number of points (`hn`) and $n > 0$ (`hn0`).
--
--   *Sections.* A section $\varepsilon$ of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and sections $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ of $c_1$, $c_2$, are given, with `hε₁` asserting that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   *Relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ data.* $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model over $A$, that is a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section. The hypothesis `hrep` provides a witness that $D$ represents the sub-Picard functor of rigidified line bundles on the model which are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero (the condition `algEquivZeroCut`), rigidified along $\varepsilon$; `hsm` and `hsep` require $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to be smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change of $D$ to $k$ relative to the special fibre of the model, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the base change to $k$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` pulled back along the first projection. Similarly $D_1$, $D_2$ are $\operatorname{Pic}^0$-designations for $c_1$, $c_2$ with representability witnesses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. A morphism $\nu_2$ from the base change of $D$ to $k$ to $D_2$ over $k$ is given, characterised by `hν₂`: for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every point $a$ of the base change of $D$ over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂.some` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along $\varepsilon_2$ of the pullback, along the curve change morphism of $i_2$, of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   *Generic and geometric generic data.* The algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ carries compatible $A$- and $L$-algebra structures. The base change of the model to $L$ is required to be smooth of relative dimension $1$ (`hsmL`) and geometrically integral (`hgiL`), and the base change $\operatorname{pullback.snd}$ of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $L$ proper (`hprL`) and geometrically connected (`hgcL`). Further, $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), together with an isomorphism $e_\eta$ from $M_\eta.C$ onto the base change of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` asserts that the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and the pinning hypothesis `hMηpin` asserts that for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the function field element of $M_\eta$ obtained as the germ at the generic point of the section corresponding to $a$ has, as a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the coefficientwise image of the Laurent series of $a$ over $L$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for $g$ an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ which fixes the image of $L$ pointwise and for two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ whose images in the finite chart differ by $g$, the places attached to them by `Mη.pointEquivPlace` differ by the action of [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) of $g$.
--
--   *Hecke–diamond inputs.* `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) and `hcomm` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), the commutation of the Hecke and diamond generators over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which defines the module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129) on $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) =$ [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182). A multiplicative semiring action of $\mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ is given, compatible with the action on $L$ (`hΓA`).
--
--   *Special fibre group data.* $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9), i.e. abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$ with a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus}$ of $G.J_{0s}$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J_{0s} \to G.J_I \times G.J_E$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$ with the $k$-points of the base change of $D$ to $k$, of $D_1$ and of $D_2$; the hypotheses `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that these bijections are additive in the sense that the Poincaré bundle pulled back along a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks; and `hproj` says that the two components of $G.\mathrm{proj}$ correspond, respectively, to post-composition with the comparison morphism `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom` attached to $i_1$ and to post-composition with $\nu_2$.
--
--   *Points, Hecke endomorphisms and Galois transport.* A bijection `gpts` identifies [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, and is additive for the relative group law of `hrep.some` (`hgadd`). To each element $t$ of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $= \mathrm{MvPoly}(\mathrm{Primes} \sqcup \mathbb{N}, \mathbb{Z})$ is attached an endomorphism $\varphi(t)$ of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and to each $s \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a morphism $\tau(s)$ of $D.P$ over the $s$-twist of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$, subject to: $\varphi(t)$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law (`hφmul`); $\varphi(t)$ realises the action of $t$ on [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) through `gpts` (`hφpts`); $\tau$ is multiplicative with $\tau(1)$ the identity (`hτ1`, `hτmul`); $\tau(s)$ and $\varphi(t)$ commute (`hτφ`); and `hτpts`, which for $\sigma'$ an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ inducing $s$ on $L$ expresses `gpts` of $\sigma' \cdot x$ as $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\sigma'$ followed by `gpts` $x$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$.
--
--   *Abel–Jacobi block.* Over $L$ one is given representability `hDL` for the base change of $D$ to $L$, a morphism $aj_L$ from the base change of the model to $L$ to the base change of $D$ to $L$, with `hajLε` saying that $\varepsilon$ goes to the zero section and `hajL` saying that for every field $K'$ over $L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the curve, the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` pulled back along $x$ followed by $aj_L$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon$; a comparison morphism $k_L$ from the geometric generic fibre to the fibre over $L$ satisfying the two projection identities `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`; the bundle comparison `hPL` over $L$; a morphism $\overline{aj}$ defined by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by $k_L$, $aj_L$ and the first projection, lying over the base as in `hajbar_over`; a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M_\eta.C$ lying over $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and sent by $\overline{aj}$ to the zero section (`hεbar_aj`); and the hypothesis `hpts_aj`: for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) equal to the difference of the places attached to $x$ and to $s$ with multiplicity one, such that `gpts` of the class of $Dv$ is $x$ followed by $\overline{aj}$.
--
--   *The norm-free abelian subscheme.* Finally, a scheme $\mathcal{A}$ with a structure morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a morphism $\iota$ from $\mathcal{A}$ to $D.P$ over $a$ are given, subject to the conjunction `h𝒜` of the clauses: $\iota$ is a closed immersion; $a$ is proper and smooth and all its geometric fibres are connected; $\iota$ is a subgroup: for every base $s : T \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ the unit of the relative group law of `hrep.some` at $s$ factors through $\iota$, and the product of two points of $\mathcal{A}$ over $s$, and the inverse of one, again factor through $\iota$; an element $x$ of [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29), the image of the norm-free endomorphism attached to the representatives at $p$, if and only if `gpts` $x$ factors through $\iota$; and $\mathcal{A}$ is stable under each $\varphi(t)$, in the sense that for every $t$, every base $s$ and every point $x$ of $\mathcal{A}$ over $s$, the point $x$ followed by $\iota$ followed by $\varphi(t)$ factors through $\iota$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses, the conclusion is the following. For every commutative ring $T'$ which is a $k$-algebra and every point $v$ of the base change of $D$ to $k$ over $\operatorname{Spec} T' \to \operatorname{Spec} k$: if there exists $z : \operatorname{Spec} T' \to \mathcal{A}$ with $z$ followed by $a$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec} T' \to \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ and with $v$ followed by the first projection equal to $z$ followed by $\iota$, then
--   $$(v \text{ followed by } \operatorname{pullback.fst}) \text{ followed by } \varphi\Big(\sum_{b \in (\mathbb{Z}/p)^\times} \langle d_b \rangle\Big)$$
--   is the unit section of the relative group law of `hrep.some` over the base $\operatorname{Spec} T' \to \operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$. Here $\langle d \rangle$ denotes [`ModularCurve.diamondGen d`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L20), the diamond generator indexed by $d$ in [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16), and $d_b$ is the natural number representative of the element of $\mathbb{Z}/Mp$ corresponding under the Chinese remainder isomorphism furnished by $p \nmid M$ to the pair $(b, 1) \in \mathbb{Z}/p \times \mathbb{Z}/M$, so that $d_b \equiv b \pmod p$ and $d_b \equiv 1 \pmod M$.
--
--   This is the statement that the sum of the diamond operators $\langle d \rangle$ over the classes $d \equiv b \bmod p$, $d \equiv 1 \bmod M$, annihilates the norm-free abelian subscheme $\mathcal{A}$ of the relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over $A$, including on its special fibre over the algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, where it is formulated as a statement about arbitrary $T'$-valued points factoring through $\iota$. It feeds the construction of the semistable specialisation family at $p$ used in the level-lowering step, being cited by [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div).
